     eexx/vvii supports regular expressions (REs), as documented in re_format(7),
     for line addresses, as the first part of the eexx ssuubbssttiittuuttee, gglloobbaall and vv
     commands, and in search patterns.  Basic regular expressions (BREs) are
     enabled by default; extended regular expressions (EREs) are used if the
     eexxtteennddeedd option is enabled.  The use of regular expressions can be
     largely disabled using the mmaaggiicc option.

     The following strings have special meanings in the eexx/vvii version of regu‐
     lar expressions:

     ••   An empty regular expression is equivalent to the last regular expres‐
         sion used.

     ••   ‘\<’ matches the beginning of the word.

     ••   ‘\>’ matches the end of the word.

     ••   ‘~’ matches the replacement part of the last ss command.

BBUUFFFFEERRSS
     A buffer is an area where commands can save changed or deleted text for
     later use.  vvii buffers are named with a single character preceded by a
     double quote, for example "⟨c⟩; eexx buffers are the same, but without the
     double quote.  nneexx/nnvvii permits the use of any character without another
     meaning in the position where a buffer name is expected.

     All buffers are either in _l_i_n_e _m_o_d_e or _c_h_a_r_a_c_t_e_r _m_o_d_e.  Inserting a buf‐
     fer in line mode into the text creates new lines for each of the lines it
     contains, while a buffer in character mode creates new lines for any
     lines _o_t_h_e_r than the first and last lines it contains.  The first and
     last lines are inserted at the current cursor position, becoming part of
     the current line.  If there is more than one line in the buffer, the cur‐
     rent line itself will be split.  All eexx commands which store text into
     buffers do so in line mode.  The behaviour of vvii commands depend on their
     associated motion command:

     ••   ⟨ccoonnttrrooll--AA⟩, hh, ll, ,,, 00, BB, EE, FF, TT, WW, ^^, bb, ee, ff and tt make the
         destination buffer character-oriented.

     ••   jj, ⟨ccoonnttrrooll--MM⟩, kk, '', --, GG, HH, LL, MM, __ and || make the destination
         buffer line-oriented.

     ••   $$, %%, ``, ((, )), //, ??, [[[[, ]]]], {{ and }} make the destination buffer
         character-oriented, unless the starting and end positions are the
         first and last characters on a line.  In that case, the buffer is
         line-oriented.

     The eexx command ddiissppllaayy bbuuffffeerrss displays the current mode for each buffer.

     Buffers named ‘a’ through ‘z’ may be referred to using their uppercase
     equivalent, in which case new content will be appended to the buffer, in‐
     stead of replacing it.

     Buffers named ‘1’ through ‘9’ are special.  A region of text modified us‐
     ing the cc (change) or dd (delete) commands is placed into the numeric buf‐
     fer ‘1’ if no other buffer is specified and if it meets one of the fol‐
     lowing conditions:

     ••   It includes characters from more than one line.

     ••   It is specified using a line-oriented motion.

     ••   It is specified using one of the following motion commands:
         ⟨ccoonnttrrooll--AA⟩, ``⟨cchhaarraacctteerr⟩, nn, NN, %%, //, {{, }}, ((, )), and ??.

     Before this copy is done, the previous contents of buffer ‘1’ are moved
     into buffer ‘2’, ‘2’ into buffer ‘3’, and so on.  The contents of buffer
     ‘9’ are discarded.  Note that this rotation occurs _r_e_g_a_r_d_l_e_s_s of the user
     specifying another buffer.  In vvii, text may be explicitly stored into the
     numeric buffers.  In this case, the buffer rotation occurs before the re‐
     placement of the buffer's contents.  The numeric buffers are only avail‐
     able in vvii mode.

